~ Atomic bindings (see docs/concurrency/README.md#sharing-state-across-fibers): `@name := …`
~ declares a mutable binding the same way `:=` does, marked as one Stage 2's fiber-sharing
~ check will let more than one fiber reach. The runtime is single-threaded until Stage 2, so
~ today an atomic binding compiles and runs exactly like a `:=` binding — its reassignment is
~ trivially atomic with only one fiber ever touching it. `@` marks the DECLARATION only; every
~ read and reassignment after that is bare, the same rule a plain `:=` binding already follows.

@yakShaves := 0     ~ a top-level atomic global — one cell every call below shares

groom = (chore :: Text) -> Text => <
  yakShaves := yakShaves + 1    ~ bare after its declaration, like any `:=` reassignment
  chore
>

^ = () -> Num => <
  groom("brush the yak")
  groom("trim the yak")
  groom("photograph the yak")
  assert(yakShaves, equals(3))

  ~ The same syntax declares a local, wherever a `:=` declaration is allowed today.
  @sideQuests := 0
  sideQuests := sideQuests + 1
  assert(sideQuests, equals(1))

  0
>
